This story starts plainly.

It was the year 3456 in the Human Era calendar. In what was known as Cairo, old scholars were building up their
knowledge of mathematics. Far before any human records existed. One such scholar, going by the name of Kushim
was trying to come up with a new way of counting barley, many thousands of years before his Uruk cousin who
was an accountant.

The key to any great civilisation is education, and Kushim's little village had plenty of that. In fact, he was
expected to teach all the little kids in Uruk the very basics of addition, subtraction, multiplication
and division.

Kushim believed in games as a way of teaching, so he devised a clever little game for teaching division. The
children would sit in a circle, with each one taking turns to count incrementally, replacing any number
divisible by three with the "fizz", and any number divisible by five with the word "buzz". With any number
divisible by both three and five being replaced by the word "fizzbuzz". These classes have been so successful
that he has given them over 100 times.

